  "provider_query": "English Wikipedia HTTP article: the default TCP port",
  "answer": "The default TCP port for HTTP is **80** (unencrypted). HTTPS uses **443**.\n\nRelevant English Wikipedia sources:\n\n1. **HTTP** — TCP/IP connections use well-known ports, typically **port 80** if unencrypted or **443** if encrypted. An example request targets `www.example.com` on port 80. HTTP/1.x and HTTP/2 use TCP; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP\n\n2. **Port (computer networking)** — By default, HTTP uses port **80** and HTTPS uses port **443**. Port numbers in URLs are omitted unless non-default (e.g. `:8080`). Table lists 80 and 8080 for HTTP.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_(computer_networking)\n\n3. **List of TCP and UDP port numbers** — Port **80**: Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP); uses TCP in versions 1.x and 2 (HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP). Port **443** is HTTPS.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_TCP_and_UDP_port_numbers\n\n4. **Port 80** — Disambiguation: TCP port 80 is most often used by the Hypertext Transfer Protocol.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_80\n\n5. **Registered port** — Ports 0–1023 are system/well-known ports (the range that includes HTTP’s default).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Registered_port\n\n6. **HTTP tunnel** — Notes that proxies often allow the default HTTPS port **443** when tunneling TLS.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP_tunnel\n\n7. **Ephemeral port** — Contrasts client ephemeral ports with servers listening on well-known ports (such as HTTP’s).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ephemeral_port\n\n8. **Open port** — Describes TCP/UDP ports that accept packets so services (e.g. web) can be reached; does not itself state the HTTP number.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_port",
